1.Which attribute specifies the media type of the linked resource?
A)rel
B)media //answer
C)type   
D)kind

Correct Answer: C. type

Explanation (Simple)

The type attribute specifies the MIME type (media type) of the linked resource.

Example:

<link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css" type="text/css">

Here:

rel="stylesheet" → Defines the relationship.
href="style.css" → Specifies the file location.
type="text/css" → Specifies the media (MIME) type of the linked resource.
Why not the others?
❌ A. rel → Defines the relationship (e.g., stylesheet, icon).
❌ B. media → Specifies which devices/media the resource applies to (e.g., screen, print).
✅ C. type → Specifies the media/MIME type (e.g., text/css).
❌ D. kind → Used with <track>, not <link>.

✅ Correct Answer: C. type


2.Which rel value tells search engines not to pass authority to the linked page?
A)noreferrer
B)external
C)nofollow //answer
D)opener

✅ Correct Answer: C. nofollow

Explanation (Simple)

The rel="nofollow" value tells search engines not to pass authority (link equity or "link juice") to the linked page.

Example:

<a href="https://example.com" rel="nofollow">
  Visit Example
</a>
Why not the others?
❌ A. noreferrer → Prevents the browser from sending the referring page information.
❌ B. external → Indicates the link is to an external site; it does not affect SEO.
✅ C. nofollow → Tells search engines not to pass authority to the linked page.
❌ D. opener → Related to browser window behavior, not SEO.

✅ Correct Answer: C. nofollow

